{
  "sim_type": "excel",
  "title": "Forensic Screening: M-Score and Z-Score Red-Flag Detector",
  "scenario": "You are a forensic analyst at a Mumbai equity research desk. Your team has asked you to screen five mid-cap Indian companies for accounting manipulation risk and bankruptcy risk using the Beneish M-Score and Altman Z-Score. You have five years of financial data (FY2019–FY2023) for each company and must compute key ratios, calculate both forensic scores, and flag which companies warrant deeper investigation.",
  "instructions": "Compute Beneish M-Score and Altman Z-Score for each company using the provided balance-sheet and P&L data; classify each company into risk zones; and identify which company shows the highest combined manipulation and bankruptcy risk.",
  "learning_objective": "Calculate Beneish M-Score and Altman Z-Score for Indian companies; interpret forensic scores responsibly as screening flags, not verdicts; and apply a structured forensic checklist to identify accounting and bankruptcy red flags.",
  "hints": [
    "M-Score uses receivables index (DSRI) and accruals (TATA) as two of its eight components; Z-Score is 1.2·(Working Capital ÷ Total Assets) + 1.4·(Retained Earnings ÷ Total Assets) + 3.3·(EBIT ÷ Total Assets) + 0.6·(Market Equity ÷ Total Liabilities) + 1.0·(Sales ÷ Total Assets).",
    "DSRI = (Receivables Year N / Sales Year N) / (Receivables Year N−1 / Sales Year N−1); TATA = (ΔCurrent Assets − ΔCash − ΔDepreciation − ΔCurrent Liabilities) / Total Assets; use SUMIFS or simple division formulas.",
    "For a simplified M-Score here, compute DSRI and TATA only (the two heaviest-weighted manipulation signals); Z-Score needs all five components from the Data sheet; flag M-Score > −1.78 and Z-Score < 1.81 as red zones."
  ],
